# Fetch Data in a Server Component

Server Components in Next.js can fetch data directly using async/await. Let's create a blog page that fetches posts from an API.

Create `app/blog/page.tsx` with the following code:

```tsx
export default async function Page() {
  const data = await fetch('https://api.vercel.app/blog')
  const posts = await data.json()
  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

**Key Points:**
- Components are `async` by default in the App Router
- `fetch` responses are not cached by default but the route output is pre-rendered
- No need for `useEffect` or client-side data fetching libraries

Test it by running:
```bash
npm run dev
```

Visit `http://localhost:3000/blog` to see the fetched posts.

# Fetch Data with an ORM or Database

Since Server Components run on the server, you can directly query databases. This example uses Drizzle ORM.

First, set up the database utilities (simulated for this tutorial):

Create `lib/db.ts`:
```tsx
// Mock database setup for demonstration
export const db = {
  select: () => ({
    from: (table: any) => Promise.resolve([
      { id: 1, title: 'First Post' },
      { id: 2, title: 'Second Post' }
    ])
  })
}

export const posts = {}
```

Update `app/blog/page.tsx`:
```tsx
import { db, posts } from '@/lib/db'

export default async function Page() {
  const allPosts = await db.select().from(posts)
  return (
    <ul>
      {allPosts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

**Important:** In production, you'd install a real ORM (Drizzle, Prisma) and configure a database connection.

# Stream Data to Client Components

You can stream data from Server to Client Components using React's `use` hook and Suspense.

First, create a data fetching function in `lib/data.ts`:
```tsx
export async function getPosts() {
  const data = await fetch('https://api.vercel.app/blog')
  return data.json()
}
```

Update `app/blog/page.tsx` (Server Component):
```tsx
import Posts from '@/app/ui/posts'
import { Suspense } from 'react'
import { getPosts } from '@/lib/data'

export default function Page() {
  // Don't await - pass the promise directly
  const posts = getPosts()

  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
      <Posts posts={posts} />
    </Suspense>
  )
}
```

Create `app/ui/posts.tsx` (Client Component):
```tsx
'use client'
import { use } from 'react'

export default function Posts({
  posts,
}: {
  posts: Promise<{ id: string; title: string }[]>
}) {
  const allPosts = use(posts)

  return (
    <ul>
      {allPosts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

The `<Suspense>` boundary shows the fallback while data loads.

# Granular Streaming with Suspense

For more control, use `<Suspense>` to stream specific parts of your page.

Create `components/BlogList.tsx`:
```tsx
import { getPosts } from '@/lib/data'

export default async function BlogList() {
  // Simulate slow data fetch
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 2000))
  const posts = await getPosts()

  return (
    <ul className="space-y-2">
      {posts.map((post: any) => (
        <li key={post.id} className="p-2 border rounded">
          {post.title}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

Create `components/BlogListSkeleton.tsx`:
```tsx
export default function BlogListSkeleton() {
  return (
    <div className="space-y-2">
      {[1, 2, 3].map((i) => (
        <div key={i} className="h-12 bg-gray-200 animate-pulse rounded"></div>
      ))}
    </div>
  )
}
```

Update `app/blog/page.tsx`:
```tsx
import { Suspense } from 'react'
import BlogList from '@/components/BlogList'
import BlogListSkeleton from '@/components/BlogListSkeleton'

export default function BlogPage() {
  return (
    <div className="p-8">
      <header className="mb-6">
        <h1 className="text-3xl font-bold">Welcome to the Blog</h1>
        <p className="text-gray-600">Read the latest posts below.</p>
      </header>
      <main>
        <Suspense fallback={<BlogListSkeleton />}>
          <BlogList />
        </Suspense>
      </main>
    </div>
  )
}
```

Now the header renders immediately while the blog list streams in!

# Sequential Data Fetching

Sometimes one request depends on another. Let's implement an artist page where playlists depend on the artist ID.

Create `lib/data.ts` helpers:
```tsx
export async function getArtist(username: string) {
  // Simulate API call
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 1000))
  return { id: '123', name: username, bio: 'Artist bio' }
}

export async function getArtistPlaylists(artistID: string) {
  // Simulate API call
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 1000))
  return [
    { id: '1', name: 'Greatest Hits' },
    { id: '2', name: 'Acoustic Sessions' }
  ]
}
```

Create `app/artist/[username]/page.tsx`:
```tsx
import { Suspense } from 'react'
import { getArtist, getArtistPlaylists } from '@/lib/data'

async function Playlists({ artistID }: { artistID: string }) {
  const playlists = await getArtistPlaylists(artistID)

  return (
    <ul className="mt-4 space-y-2">
      {playlists.map((playlist) => (
        <li key={playlist.id} className="p-2 bg-blue-50 rounded">
          {playlist.name}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}

export default async function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ username: string }>
}) {
  const { username } = await params
  const artist = await getArtist(username)

  return (
    <div className="p-8">
      <h1 className="text-2xl font-bold">{artist.name}</h1>
      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading playlists...</div>}>
        <Playlists artistID={artist.id} />
      </Suspense>
    </div>
  )
}
```

Visit `http://localhost:3000/artist/taylor-swift` to see sequential fetching in action.

# Parallel Data Fetching

When requests are independent, fetch them in parallel for better performance using `Promise.all`.

Update `lib/data.ts`:
```tsx
export async function getAlbums(username: string) {
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 1500))
  return [
    { id: '1', title: 'Album One', year: 2020 },
    { id: '2', title: 'Album Two', year: 2022 }
  ]
}
```

Create `app/artist/[username]/albums.tsx`:
```tsx
export default function Albums({ 
  list 
}: { 
  list: { id: string; title: string; year: number }[] 
}) {
  return (
    <div className="mt-4">
      <h2 className="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Albums</h2>
      <div className="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        {list.map((album) => (
          <div key={album.id} className="p-4 border rounded">
            <div className="font-medium">{album.title}</div>
            <div className="text-sm text-gray-500">{album.year}</div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  )
}
```

Create `app/artist/[username]/parallel/page.tsx`:
```tsx
import { getArtist, getAlbums } from '@/lib/data'
import Albums from '../albums'

export default async function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ username: string }>
}) {
  const { username } = await params

  // Initiate both requests without awaiting
  const artistData = getArtist(username)
  const albumsData = getAlbums(username)

  // Wait for both to complete
  const [artist, albums] = await Promise.all([artistData, albumsData])

  return (
    <div className="p-8">
      <h1 className="text-2xl font-bold">{artist.name}</h1>
      <p className="text-gray-600">{artist.bio}</p>
      <Albums list={albums} />
    </div>
  )
}
```

Visit `http://localhost:3000/artist/taylor-swift/parallel`.

**Performance:** Parallel fetching takes ~1.5s (longest request) vs ~2.5s sequential!

# Preloading Data

Preload data by eagerly calling functions before blocking operations. This optimizes conditional rendering scenarios.

Update `lib/data.ts`:
```tsx
export async function getItem(id: string) {
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 1000))
  return {
    id,
    name: `Item ${id}`,
    description: 'A detailed item description',
    price: 99.99
  }
}

export async function checkIsAvailable() {
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 500))
  return Math.random() > 0.3 // 70% chance available
}

// Preload utility
export const preload = (id: string) => {
  void getItem(id)
}
```

Create `components/Item.tsx`:
```tsx
import { getItem } from '@/lib/data'

export async function Item({ id }: { id: string }) {
  const result = await getItem(id)

  return (
    <div className="p-6 border rounded-lg">
      <h2 className="text-xl font-bold">{result.name}</h2>
      <p className="text-gray-600 mt-2">{result.description}</p>
      <p className="text-lg font-semibold mt-4">${result.price}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

Create `app/item/[id]/page.tsx`:
```tsx
import { checkIsAvailable, preload } from '@/lib/data'
import { Item } from '@/components/Item'

export default async function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ id: string }>
}) {
  const { id } = await params

  // Start loading item data immediately
  preload(id)

  // Perform another async task
  const isAvailable = await checkIsAvailable()

  return (
    <div className="p-8">
      {isAvailable ? (
        <Item id={id} />
      ) : (
        <div className="text-red-600 font-semibold">
          This item is currently unavailable
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  )
}
```

Visit `http://localhost:3000/item/123` and refresh to see conditional rendering.

# Request Deduplication with React cache

When using ORMs or databases (not `fetch`), use React's `cache` function to deduplicate requests.

Install server-only package:
```bash
npm install server-only
```

Create `utils/get-item.ts`:
```tsx
import { cache } from 'react'
import 'server-only'

// Simulate database query
const fetchItemFromDB = async (id: string) => {
  console.log(`[DB Query] Fetching item ${id}`)
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 1000))
  return {
    id,
    name: `Database Item ${id}`,
    category: 'Electronics',
    stock: 42
  }
}

export const preload = (id: string) => {
  void getItem(id)
}

export const getItem = cache(async (id: string) => {
  return fetchItemFromDB(id)
})
```

Create `app/cached-item/[id]/page.tsx`:
```tsx
import { getItem } from '@/utils/get-item'

async function ItemDetails({ id }: { id: string }) {
  const item = await getItem(id)
  return <div className="text-gray-600">Category: {item.category}</div>
}

async function ItemStock({ id }: { id: string }) {
  const item = await getItem(id)
  return <div className="text-green-600">Stock: {item.stock}</div>
}

export default async function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ id: string }>
}) {
  const { id } = await params
  const item = await getItem(id)

  return (
    <div className="p-8">
      <h1 className="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">{item.name}</h1>
      <ItemDetails id={id} />
      <ItemStock id={id} />
      <p className="mt-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        Check console - only ONE database query!
      </p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

Visit `http://localhost:3000/cached-item/456` and check the terminal - you'll see only ONE `[DB Query]` log despite three calls to `getItem`!

# Advanced Caching Options

Next.js provides several caching mechanisms for `fetch` requests.

## Force Dynamic Rendering

Create `app/dynamic-blog/page.tsx`:
```tsx
export default async function Page() {
  // Force dynamic rendering - no caching
  const data = await fetch('https://api.vercel.app/blog', {
    cache: 'no-store'
  })
  const posts = await data.json()

  return (
    <div className="p-8">
      <p className="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
        Fetched at: {new Date().toLocaleTimeString()}
      </p>
      <ul className="space-y-2">
        {posts.map((post: any) => (
          <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  )
}
```

## Force Caching

Create `app/cached-blog/page.tsx`:
```tsx
export default async function Page() {
  // Force caching - revalidate every 60 seconds
  const data = await fetch('https://api.vercel.app/blog', {
    next: { revalidate: 60 }
  })
  const posts = await data.json()

  return (
    <div className="p-8">
      <p className="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
        Cached data (revalidates every 60s)
      </p>
      <ul className="space-y-2">
        {posts.map((post: any) => (
          <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  )
}
```

**Caching Options:**
- `cache: 'no-store'` - Always fetch fresh data
- `cache: 'force-cache'` - Cache indefinitely
- `next: { revalidate: 60 }` - Cache for 60 seconds then refresh
- Default: Static rendering with cached output
